    "F-i2": {
      "object": "integer",
      "owner": "operation",
      "kind": "fracture",
      "description": "Overflow: what happens when + - * exceed the width. The census's five behaviours: wrap silently (java kotlin csharp go dart-native cpp-UNSIGNED); undefined behaviour (cpp SIGNED); mode-dependent (rust debug PANICS / release wraps; dart native wraps / web behaves as floats); become a float (php); grow (python ruby). Probe a is each language's guaranteed spelling of 'largest value plus one', through opaque runtime values so no compiler can answer it at build time. Probe b is the UNSIGNED wrap, which IS a guarantee even in c++ (and in rust is spelled wrapping_add). Probe c is rust's explicit wrapping_add, which must hold in BOTH build modes -- the guarantee, as against the mode-dependent default. cpp signed overflow expects <any>, per the owner's cpp-UB ruling: probed for evidence, but there is no promise to break.",
